      "pattern": "drift",
      "direction": "increase",
      "magnitude": "Temporary upward drift in Death A/E from ~1.08 in 2016 to ~1.25 in 2017 and ~1.52 in 2018 (peak ~+0.52 vs baseline), then reversion to ~0.73 in 2019 and mostly baseline/lower values from 2020-2024.",
      "confidence": 0.85,
      "evidence": [
        "artifacts/ae_death_by_year.csv: Death AE rises over 2016=1.084, 2017=1.248, 2018=1.522, then falls to 2019=0.734 and remains around or below baseline in 2020-2024.",
        "summary.json Death overall_ae=0.9972, so the 2016-2018 excess is largely offset by later years, consistent with a sub-window anomaly rather than a permanent level change.",
        "Empirical Death year-to-year A/E SD is materially larger than a Poisson 1/sqrt(Expected) null, so this is treated as a sub-window drift/dispersion pattern rather than a single-year Poisson outlier."
      ],
      "recommended_action": "Investigate 2016-2018 Death claims by age/gender, cause/diagnosis, claim count versus average cost, large claims, data-entry/coding errors, and expected-mortality basis. Check whether the peak is driven by a specific cohort, operational issue, or basis change. If no specific cause is found, document as temporary mortality volatility and monitor 2025+ for recurrence; consider pricing/valuation follow-up only if the period is judged to reflect a persistent adverse selection or basis error."
    }
  ]
}

